exav: THIRD-PARTY NOTICES
==========================

exav itself is licensed under the MIT License (see the LICENSE file).

The exav binary statically links a number of third-party Rust crates. Those
crates retain their own licenses; the binary distribution must carry their
notices. This file enumerates the major components and reproduces the notice
texts their BSD, zlib and MIT licenses require, and the Apache License 2.0
(section 4) for the components under it.

This is a hand-maintained summary of the principal components, not a byte-exact
dump of every transitive crate's license. To regenerate a complete, per-crate
attribution list from the resolved dependency graph, install one of:

    cargo install cargo-about        # then: cargo about generate about.hbs
    cargo install cargo-bundle-licenses

and run it against this workspace. `cargo deny check licenses` (run in CI)
confirms every dependency resolves to a permissive license on the allowlist in
deny.toml.

Versions below reflect Cargo.lock at the time of writing (last verified
2026-09-09).


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. yara-x, BSD-3-Clause (source derivative, and a development dependency)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

exav's YARA engine is native, in `crates/exav-core/src/yara/`. Parts of it are
derived from yara-x's source: the module implementations (`pe`, `elf`, `dotnet`,
`math`, `hash`, `string`) and the compiler's base64 handling, including field
semantics and the RVA-to-offset and import-hash routines. Each such file carries
a per-file attribution header, and the license text is reproduced below and in
`crates/exav-core/LICENSE-YARA-X`.

`yara-x-parser` is a regular OPTIONAL DEPENDENCY, enabled by the default-on
`yara` feature of `crates/exav-core`, so a default build does link it. No other
yara-x crate is a dependency of any kind.

The differential test that compares exav's engine against yara-x
(`crates/exav-core/tests/yara_difftest.rs`) invokes yara-x as a PROGRAM (the
`yr` binary from `PATH`) rather than linking it, and skips when that binary is
absent.

Components: yara-x-parser 1.19.
